1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,785
Every tap on your phone uses an idea from a 1965 radar lab.

2
00:00:05,118 --> 00:00:09,232
In 1965, Eric Johnson worked at a British radar lab. He wanted air

3
00:00:09,332 --> 00:00:13,445
traffic controllers to pick a flight on the screen with one touch.

4
00:00:13,785 --> 00:00:17,331
He put thin wires across the screen. Your finger changes how

5
00:00:17,431 --> 00:00:20,490
much charge a wire can hold, and a circuit feels it.

6
00:00:20,835 --> 00:00:23,060
That is the trick: touch, charge, find.

7
00:00:23,402 --> 00:00:26,125
About forty years later, phone makers removed the

8
00:00:26,225 --> 00:00:28,315
keys. The whole front became a screen.

9
00:00:28,652 --> 00:00:31,272
They used the same trick, with a grid of clear

10
00:00:31,372 --> 00:00:34,643
wires under the glass. Your finger takes charge where two

11
00:00:34,743 --> 00:00:37,128
wires cross, and the phone finds the spot.

12
00:00:37,468 --> 00:00:41,650
Then touchscreens returned to air traffic control. Today, many British

13
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:45,198
controllers move flight strips with a touch, not on paper.

14
00:00:45,535 --> 00:00:48,507
Today, most phones and tablets feel your finger this way.

15
00:00:48,852 --> 00:00:52,070
Air traffic control taught screens to feel a finger. Then

16
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:55,621
touchscreens helped guide the planes. Same trick, two worlds.

